{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce they 'reached an agreement' whose provisions were folded into the Collins-Lieberman FEMA legislation (independent status within DHS, direct access to the President, new agency name). The codebook clarification explicitly counts 'negotiated agreements folded into legislation' as credit-claiming, and Collins' quote credits Lott's input with improving the bill."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as an informational essay about the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator writes 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators' — an explicit first-person claim of securing appropriated funds, which the clarification says counts even when embedded in a release nominally about something else."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Most of the release is position-taking from Bond's chamber-of-commerce visit, but he touts 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' Claiming a delivered state benefit through legislation he co-authored is credit-claiming embedded in an otherwise general update."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as an Appropriations Committee member, announces that DHS will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department in Alabama. Under the clarification, an announcement by the senator's office of federal money benefiting the state functions as an implicit credit claim even without the words 'I secured.'"
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "DeWine announces a $105,550 grant to Parma Fire Department 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' notes he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' and that he 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for the program. This is the archetypal credit claim: announcing funds delivered to the state via his own legislation and appropriations work."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement criticizes Democrats' filibusters of judicial nominees and defends the 'constitutional option.' It is pure position-taking and criticism of the opposing party with no claim of personal responsibility for any delivered benefit or legislative product, matching negative clarification (a)."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Hutchison announces she 'signed on as an original cosponsor of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act of 2007' and touts what the bill would do. The clarification explicitly counts announcements of cosponsored bills as credit-claiming even though the bill is only introduced."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez states he cannot support the Pombo OCS bill, applauds Congressman Putnam's efforts (crediting another actor), and threatens to join a filibuster unless the buffer is extended. This is position-taking on someone else's bill plus a demand — no accomplished outcome or own legislative product is claimed (negative clarifications a and b)."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces $8.1 million included in the FY2006 MilCon/VA appropriations bill for Portsmouth Naval Shipyard, noting the 'funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' plus veterans funding she 'supported throughout the appropriations process.' This matches the classic pattern of claiming credit for appropriated funds delivered to the home state."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release is nominally an essay-contest announcement (ceremonial), but it embeds an accomplishment claim: 'One of Senator Bayh's first pieces of legislation in the Senate was the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into law... The Act was authored...' and touts his gubernatorial record. The clarification explicitly says to count accomplishment claims embedded in an essay-contest release."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords merely explains his vote in support of the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration. Explaining a vote on others' amendments is pure position-taking under negative clarification (a); no personal responsibility for any outcome is claimed."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Nelson announces that his own amendment passed the Senate 72-26, calling it 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.' This is the codebook's own positive example: a senator claiming personal responsibility for an adopted amendment benefiting his state's cattle producers."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's statement introduces his own bill: 'Today I am introducing legislation to extend Medicare to all Americans... I call this approach Medicare for All.' The clarification states that claims about the senator's own legislation count even if the bill is only introduced or pending; much of the speech touts his proposal."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release centers on an upcoming hearing (normally advocacy in progress), it claims accomplished oversight results: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415%... levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Levin chairs and Coleman is ranking member of the subcommittee, and the release frames these recoveries as the product of their investigation — matching the definition's 'claiming results (e.g., recoveries) from one's oversight' and negative clarification (b)'s exception for releases that also claim an accomplished result."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces the 'Kyl amendment' to the Senate Budget Resolution that Kyl co-sponsored, calling for $352 million and 2,000 new Border Patrol agents. Sponsoring/cosponsoring and touting one's own amendment counts as credit-claiming per the clarification, regardless of whether it has passed."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The main news — a letter to Secretary Chertoff and requests for appropriations — is advocacy in progress. However, the release also states 'Sen. Cornyn added an amendment to the Department of State and Foreign Operations Appropriations bill that required the IBWC... to report to Congress,' an accomplished legislative action (adopted amendment). Per the clarification, accomplishment claims (adopted amendments) embedded in a release nominally about ongoing advocacy count as credit-claiming."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Murkowski introduces her own bill to change Alaska's FMAP formula and also claims a past delivered result: she 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million.' Both introducing her own legislation and claiming a previously won funding benefit for the state are credit-claiming."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces FEMA is moving ~300 trailers and 100 mobile homes to Mississippi for Katrina victims, and notes 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport.' This fits clarification (a): a favorable federal action benefiting the state presented as following the senator's personal appeal, i.e., an implicit claim of responsibility."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison describes questioning an Army official at a hearing, receiving assurances that BRAC is on schedule, and expressing support for 'Grow the Force' funding. She says she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood' but does not claim to have secured it. This is oversight activity and support in progress with no delivered outcome claimed (negative clarification b), akin to the Dodd/Lieberman BRAC negative example."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release urges conferees to retain the provision (advocacy in progress), it also claims an accomplished result: 'the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty, repeatedly called 'his legislation.' An adopted amendment to one's credit is credit-claiming even while further advocacy continues."
  }
}
